  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn elongated sfc to mid level ridge axis extending across the Great \nLakes region today through Wednesday will maintain the persistent \ndry pattern across the forecast area. A northern stream short wave \nforecast to track across northern Ontario Wednesday will drive a \nweakening sfc cold front into the Great Lakes region Wed night. This \nfrontal boundary will essentially wash out as long wave ridge \namplification takes hold across the central US Thurs into Friday,\nresulting in rebounding mid level heights across Lower Mi. This will\nstrengthen the large low-mid level anticyclone across much of the \neastern US, the influence of which will affect Se Mi through Saturday\nsustaining dry conditions with relatively little cloud cover. \n\nThe dry ambient airmass will continue to support relatively cool \nnights with ample afternoon sunshine today through Saturday. \nForecast mixing depths and thermal profiles remain supportive of \nafternoon highs from the mid 60s to lower 70s today through \nThursday. Sfc high pressure is forecast to strengthen to 1035mb \nacross Quebec by Friday. This may drive a slightly cooler airmass \nacross the eastern Great Lakes, warranting a little lower forecast \nhighs on Friday. With the center of the sfc high pressure already \ncentered across NE Ontario and Quebec, light easterly winds off the \nlakes will contribute to some enhanced fog potential. There are some \nindications in the hi res suite for some degree of fog expansion \nacross the thumb this morning. A more notable moisture push tonight \ninto Wed morning may allow a more expansive advective fog event off \nLake Erie, with the stronger easterly flow possibly supporting the \nbetter fog chances farther west toward Adrian and Ann Arbor. \n\nThere is indication in Ecwmf and GFS ensemble members that a forecast \ndeeper upper wave tracking across central Canada Sunday into early \nnext week will break down the ridging across the Great Lakes, \nsuggesting a possible pattern change for next week.",
